## 2. L1 invariant → fixture (19 invariants, 0 orphans)

| Invariant | Rule | Invalid fixture(s) | Valid counter-example |
|---|---|---|---|
| `INV-DEL-1` | `status` enum has no `DELETED` | `I08` | all |
| `INV-DEL-2` | `superseded_by` iff `SUPERSEDED` | `I12` | V07, V08 |
| `INV-DEL-3` | `retraction` iff `RETRACTED` | `I10`, `I13` | V09, V10 |
| `INV-DEL-4` | no unknown top-level property | `I09` | all |
| `INV-CONF-1` | `confidence` is an object | `I01` | all |
| `INV-CONF-2` | `method` ∈ closed enum, no authored member | `I02` | V04, V05, V16 |
| `INV-CONF-3` | `inputs` `minItems: 1` | `I03` | all |
| `INV-CONF-4` | `computed_by` names a component | `I21` | all |
| `INV-CONF-5` | `value` ∈ [0,1] | `I04` | all |
| `INV-PROMO-1` | `basis` ∈ closed epistemic enum | `I05` | V03-V16 |
| `INV-PROMO-2` | `promotion.evidence_refs` `minItems: 1` | `I07` | V03-V16 |
| `INV-PROMO-3` | `promotion` required iff `status != PROPOSED` | `I18` | V01 (absent), V13 (present on PROPOSED) |
| `INV-PROMO-4` | `promotion` rejects unknown properties | `I06` | all |
| `INV-FSM-1` | `(from,to)` is a legal edge; `RETRACTED` absorbing | `I11` | V03-V16 |
| `INV-FSM-2` | `basis` permitted on that edge | `I17` | V03-V16 |
| `INV-EV-1` | `evidence_refs` non-empty unless `PROPOSED` | `I19` | V01, V03-V16 |
| `INV-QUAR-1` | `quarantine` iff `QUARANTINED` | `I22` | V11 |
| `INV-DEP-1` | `deprecation` iff `DEPRECATED` | `I23` | V06 |
| `INV-TZ-1` | `Z0` requires `actor_role: founder` | `I20` | V15 |

**Mechanised by:** `test_every_invariant_has_invalid_fixture` scans every `$comment` in the schema
with `re.findall(r"INV-[A-Z]+-\d+", ...)` — not an anchored match, because `promotion`'s single
`$comment` declares both `INV-PROMO-2` and `INV-PROMO-4` (`design.md` §3.1) — and asserts each id
found is claimed by ≥1 manifest entry. Adding an invariant
to the schema without a fixture fails the suite. This is the mechanism that makes "every §4.2
invariant violation is exercised" a *checked* claim.

**Also mechanised:** every invariant row has a valid counter-example. A rule that only ever appears
in rejections might be rejecting everything; the paired valid fixture proves the rule discriminates
rather than merely blocks — the `CANARY-0002` concern applied to the schema itself
(`constitution/canaries/CANARY-0002.json:4`: "the same PASS for the claim and its negation").

